     CoffeeLiqueur`Extensions`TOC`Private`heading[2, "Animation  ", 
      CoffeeLiqueur`Notebook`Cells`CellObj[
       CoffeeLiqueur`Notebook`Cells`CellObj`$420]]}|>, 
 "Cells" -> {<|"Data" -> ".md\n# Modeling Earth\n*using GeoData, surface \
plot, and a bunch of other beautiful stuff*\n\n*written by @JerryI*\n\nHave \
you ever played Outer Wilds? The planets there are incredibly beautiful. This \
actually became the main motivation to create my own simple model of a \
planet, using real geographical elevation data and a bit of Wolfram Language \
magic.", "Display" -> "codemirror", 
    "Hash" -> "44263d92-4006-4a55-b78b-a98d4ff25c8f", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <|"Hidden" -> True|>, 
    "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Input", 
    "UID" -> Null, "Notebook" -> "974e939a-7268-4f58-b7f9-95898cc3d9dd"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> "# Modeling Earth\n*using GeoData, surface plot, and a bunch \
of other beautiful stuff*\n\n*written by @JerryI*\n\nHave you ever played \
Outer Wilds? The planets there are incredibly beautiful. This actually became \
the main motivation to create my own simple model of a planet, using real \
geographical elevation data and a bit of Wolfram Language magic.", 
    "Display" -> "markdown", "Hash" -> 
     "23a975d5-5009-4046-986e-bba8a8eaebd4", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <||>, "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, 
    "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Output", "UID" -> Null, 
    "Notebook" -> "974e939a-7268-4f58-b7f9-95898cc3d9dd"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> ".md\n## Spreading points on a sphere\n\nObviously, even if we \
grab Earth's elevation map, there\[CloseCurlyQuote]s the problem of \
projecting it onto a sphere. This is a complex topic involving triangulation \
(since we need to create polygons from it afterward) and more, with plenty \
written on the subject. For example:\n\n- [Delaunay and Voronoi on a \
Sphere](https://www.redblobgames.com/x/1842-delaunay-voronoi-sphere/)  \n- \
[Four Ways to Create a Mesh for a \
Sphere](https://medium.com/@oscarsc/four-ways-to-create-a-mesh-for-a-sphere-d\
7956b825db4)  \n\nAs one approach, we\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll go the other way \
around\[LongDash]first generating sample points on the sphere, then querying \
the elevation data for those points.  \n\nSo, we need to evenly distribute \
**N** points across a sphere. No need to reinvent the wheel when a rich \
standard library is available.  ", "Display" -> "codemirror", 
    "Hash" -> "24de6106-924f-417d-93cb-762b31d9ec58", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <|"Hidden" -> True|>, 
    "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Input", 
    "UID" -> Null, "Notebook" -> "974e939a-7268-4f58-b7f9-95898cc3d9dd"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> "## Spreading points on a sphere\n\nObviously, even if we grab \
Earth's elevation map, there\[CloseCurlyQuote]s the problem of projecting it \
onto a sphere. This is a complex topic involving triangulation (since we need \
to create polygons from it afterward) and more, with plenty written on the \
subject. For example:\n\n- [Delaunay and Voronoi on a \
Sphere](https://www.redblobgames.com/x/1842-delaunay-voronoi-sphere/)  \n- \
[Four Ways to Create a Mesh for a \
Sphere](https://medium.com/@oscarsc/four-ways-to-create-a-mesh-for-a-sphere-d\
7956b825db4)  \n\nAs one approach, we\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll go the other way \
around\[LongDash]first generating sample points on the sphere, then querying \
the elevation data for those points.  \n\nSo, we need to evenly distribute \
**N** points across a sphere. No need to reinvent the wheel when a rich \
standard library is available.  ", "Display" -> "markdown", 
